// What the screens say about each weekly portion of Genesis (Bereshit). One entry per portion:
//   meaning   what the portion's name means (or, for a portion named after a person, who: who they are)
//   summary   what happens, in plain English
//   lessons   its main lessons (the left screen shows four), each with where it comes from
//   days      one insight per section (aliyah), Sunday to Shabbat. "verse" is the verse it's about, which
//             the right screen shows with Rashi and the Talmud on it; it has to be inside that day's section.
window.TORAH_LESSONS = window.TORAH_LESSONS || {};
Object.assign(window.TORAH_LESSONS, {

  'Bereshit': {
    meaning: 'In the beginning',
    summary: 'God creates the world in six days and rests on the seventh. Adam and Eve live in the Garden of Eden until they eat from the forbidden tree and are sent out. Their son Cain kills his brother Abel. Ten generations later the world is full of wrongdoing, but one man, Noah, finds favor with God.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Every person is made in God’s image', text: 'The Torah begins with all of humanity, not one nation. If every person carries God’s image, every life is worth everything. The Mishnah says that’s why the world began with one person: no one can claim a greater ancestor.', source: 'Genesis 1:27 · Mishnah Sanhedrin 4:5' },
      { title: 'Rest is part of creation', text: 'After six days of making, God stops and makes the seventh day holy. Shabbat says we are more than what we produce. Stopping isn’t quitting; it’s how the work becomes complete.', source: 'Genesis 2:1–3' },
      { title: '“Where are you?”', text: 'After eating the fruit, Adam hides, and God calls out: “Where are you?” God knew. The question is for Adam, and for us: change starts when we stop hiding and say where we really are.', source: 'Genesis 3:9' },
      { title: 'We are our brothers’ keepers', text: 'Cain asks, “Am I my brother’s keeper?” The rest of the Torah answers yes. And before the murder, God told Cain he could master his anger: the urge is real, but so is the choice.', source: 'Genesis 4:7–9' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 1:31', headline: 'The rabbis say “very good” includes the urge to do wrong.', text: 'Each day God sees that what was made is “good.” At the end of the sixth day, looking at everything, it is “very good.” The Midrash reads the phrase twice: “behold, it was very good” is the good inclination; “<i>and</i> behold, it was very good” is the <i>yetzer hara</i>, the urge to do wrong. Very good? “Were it not for the evil inclination, a man would never build a house, marry a wife, have children, or do business” (Bereshit Rabbah 9:7). The drive that can ruin you is also the engine of everything you build.', try: 'Name the ambition or appetite you’re most uneasy about in yourself. Instead of only fighting it today, aim it at one thing worth building.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 2:18', headline: 'A person who needs no one is playing God.', text: '“It is not good for the man to be alone” is the first thing in creation God calls not good. Rashi gives a surprising reason, from the Midrash: alone, Adam would look like a second god, the one being below without a partner, as God is the one above. Needing no one is a claim to be God. And the partner God makes is an <i>ezer kenegdo</i>, literally “a help against him.” Rashi: if he deserves it, she helps him; if not, she stands against him and fights him. Real help sometimes arrives as opposition.', try: 'Ask someone for help today with something you’d normally insist on handling alone, and let them push back on how you do it.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 3:21', headline: 'After the first sin, God sews them clothes.', text: 'Adam and Eve have disobeyed, hidden and blamed each other, and their sentence has been spoken. Then, before sending them out, “the LORD God made garments of skin for Adam and his wife, and clothed them.” Rabbi Simlai in the Talmud says the Torah begins and ends with kindness: it opens with God clothing the naked and closes with God burying Moses (Sotah 14a). The Midrash adds that Rabbi Meir’s Torah scroll read “garments of <i>light</i>” (Bereshit Rabbah 20:12). The first thing God does for people who have just failed is cover their shame.', try: 'Think of someone who has just messed up in front of you or others. Do one practical thing today that covers them instead of correcting them.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 4:16', headline: 'Adam learned about repentance from the son who killed his brother.', text: '“Cain went out from the presence of the LORD.” The Midrash is divided about him: some rabbis say he left like someone trying to fool God. But another tells this story. Adam meets Cain and asks what became of his sentence. Cain says, “I repented, and made my peace.” Adam strikes his own face: “So great is the power of repentance, and I didn’t know!” Then he sings the psalm for Shabbat, “It is good to give thanks to the LORD” (Bereshit Rabbah 22:13). The father never knew a person could come back. The murderer showed him.', try: 'Pick one thing you’ve decided is too late to fix. Take one real step toward repairing it today.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 4:22', headline: 'The first music and the first blades come from Cain’s family.', text: 'This short section names the founders of culture: Jabal, the first herdsman; Jubal, the first musician; Tubal-cain, the first smith. All of them descend from Cain, six generations down from the first murderer. Rashi reads Tubal-cain’s name as “he refined the craft of Cain”: he perfected Cain’s work by making weapons for killers. The Torah doesn’t pretend progress is innocent. The same forge makes plows and swords. Yet Rashi adds that their sister Naamah became Noah’s wife, so something from this family goes into the ark.', try: 'Look at the skill you’re best at, your words, your mind, your craft, and ask honestly at the end of today: did it build something, or cut someone?' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 5:1', headline: 'Ben Azzai found the Torah’s greatest rule in a genealogy.', text: 'Rabbi Akiva says the Torah’s great principle is “Love your neighbor as yourself.” Ben Azzai picks a dry-looking verse instead: “This is the book of the generations of Adam… in the likeness of God He made him.” Why? The Midrash warns against saying, “Since I was shamed, let my fellow be shamed with me,” and a person who thinks little of himself can love his neighbor “as himself” in exactly that way. Rabbi Tanchuma answers: if you do, know whom you are shaming, one made “in the likeness of God” (Bereshit Rabbah 24:7).', try: 'The next time today someone makes you feel small and you’re tempted to pass it down the line, to a waiter, a coworker, a sibling, stop it with you.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 6:6', headline: 'God made people knowing they would break His heart.', text: 'The world has gone bad, and the Torah says something astonishing: God “regretted making man, and was grieved in His heart.” Rashi quotes a debate from the Midrash. A man asks Rabbi Yehoshua ben Korcha: doesn’t God know the future? Then how can He grieve? The rabbi asks: when your son was born, didn’t you celebrate, though you knew he would someday die? The man answers: “At a time of joy, joy; at a time of mourning, mourning.” So with God: He knew how it would go, and created us anyway, for the sake of the righteous who would come.', try: 'Call someone you’re afraid of losing someday, a parent, an old friend, and enjoy them today without rehearsing the ending.' },
    ],
  },

  'Noach': {
    who: 'Noah, whose name means “rest”',
    summary: 'The world is full of violence, so God brings a flood. Noah, the one righteous man of his time, builds an ark and saves his family and the animals. Afterward God promises never to destroy the world again, with the rainbow as a sign. Later, people build the Tower of Babel and are scattered, and the portion ends with the family of Abram.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Righteous in his time', text: 'Noah was “righteous in his generation.” The rabbis argued over that phrase: maybe he’d have been even greater in a better time, or maybe he only looked good next to the wicked (Rashi). Either way, we’re measured in the times we live in.', source: 'Genesis 6:9 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Violence ruins the world', text: 'The flood comes because “the earth was filled with <i>chamas</i>”: violence and lawlessness. The Talmud says the final decree was sealed over robbery. How we treat each other is what holds the world together.', source: 'Genesis 6:11–13 · Sanhedrin 108a' },
      { title: 'A promise in the clouds', text: 'After the flood God makes a covenant with every living thing, not only Noah’s family, and sets the rainbow as its sign. God commits to the world even knowing people will fail again.', source: 'Genesis 9:8–17' },
      { title: 'The trouble with Babel', text: 'The builders of Babel want to “make a name” for themselves with a tower to the sky: one language, one plan, no room for anyone different. God scatters them into many peoples. Difference isn’t the punishment; it’s the plan.', source: 'Genesis 11:1–9' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 6:14', headline: 'The ark was a 120-year warning that nobody heeded.', text: 'God could have saved Noah a hundred ways. Why make him build a huge boat? Rashi, from the Talmud: so that people would watch him at it for 120 years and ask, “What is this for?” He would tell them a flood was coming, and perhaps they would change (Sanhedrin 108b). They watched. No one changed. The ark is the Torah’s picture of a warning in plain sight: people can look at the same slow-building truth for a lifetime and still be surprised when the water comes.', try: 'Name the “ark” going up in your own life, the warning in your health, money or a relationship that you’ve watched for years. Do one concrete thing about it today.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 7:7', headline: 'Even the one righteous man waited until his feet were wet.', text: 'God tells Noah to go into the ark, and he does, “because of the waters of the flood.” Rashi, from the Midrash, reads it sharply: Noah “believed and did not believe” that the flood would come, and didn’t go in until the water forced him (Bereshit Rabbah 32:6). This is the man the Torah calls righteous and whole. Then, nine verses later: “and the LORD shut him in.” Noah’s faith got him only as far as the door; God closed it behind him. You don’t need complete certainty to be carried.', try: 'Take the step you only half believe in today, the application, the conversation, the appointment, without waiting until you’re sure.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 8:11', headline: 'The dove chose a bitter olive over honey from someone’s hand.', text: 'After months on the water, Noah sends out a dove, and in the evening it comes back with “a plucked olive leaf in its mouth.” Why an olive, of all things? The Talmud hears the dove speaking: “Let my food be bitter as an olive, but from the hand of God, rather than sweet as honey and from the hand of flesh and blood” (Eruvin 18b, quoted by Rashi). After months of eating from Noah’s hand, the first thing it chooses outside is to owe no human being its bread, even if it tastes worse.', try: 'Find one place where you depend on someone’s favor more than you’d like, a boss, a parent, a person you keep pleasing. Take one small step today toward standing on your own there.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 8:21', headline: 'The flaw that caused the flood becomes the reason for mercy.', text: 'Before the flood, God sees that every plan of the human heart is “only evil, all day long,” and decides to wipe the world out. After it, smelling Noah’s offering, God promises never to curse the ground again, “because the inclination of man’s heart is evil from his youth.” Same diagnosis, opposite conclusion. Rashi says that urge is there from the moment a baby stirs to leave the womb (Bereshit Rabbah 34:10). People haven’t changed after the flood; what changes is what God expects. The new world is built to hold flawed people.', try: 'Pick one person whose flaw you keep hoping will disappear. Today, plan around it with patience instead of waiting for them to change.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 9:12', headline: 'A rainbow is a reprieve, not a reward.', text: 'God makes the rainbow the sign of the covenant “for all generations to come.” Rashi notices that the word for generations is spelled short, missing a letter, and explains from the Midrash: some generations won’t need the sign at all, because they are completely righteous, like the generation of King Hezekiah and that of Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai (Bereshit Rabbah 35:2). So the rainbow appears over a world that deserves judgment and is spared anyway. It’s beautiful, and it’s a quiet rebuke. Seeing one means we are living on mercy.', try: 'Think of a time you got a second chance you didn’t earn. Give one away today: let a mistake, a debt or a late reply go without comment.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 9:20', headline: 'The man who survived the end of the world planted a vineyard.', text: 'Off the ark, Noah “began to be a man of the soil, and planted a vineyard,” then drank and lay exposed in his tent. Rashi reads “began,” <i>vayachel</i>, as “he made himself profane”: he should have planted something else first. The Midrash Tanchuma imagines Satan as his partner, watering the vines with the blood of a lamb, a lion, a pig and an ape: a drinker starts meek, turns boastful, then crude, then foolish. The Torah doesn’t say why Noah drank. It only shows a man who has just watched the world drown reaching first for wine.', try: 'Notice what you reach for first after a hard day. Tonight, swap it once for something that actually restores you.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 11:32', headline: 'The Torah reports Terah’s death sixty years early.', text: 'The portion ends “and Terah died in Haran,” and next God tells Abram to go. But Rashi does the math: Terah was 70 when Abram was born, Abram leaves at 75, and Terah dies at 205. His father lived sixty more years. Why tell of his death first? So people wouldn’t say Abram failed to honor his father by leaving him in his old age (Bereshit Rabbah 39:7). And the Torah may call him dead, Rashi adds, because the wicked are called dead even while alive. The Torah’s first great journey begins with a son leaving his aging father.', try: 'If you’ve put distance between yourself and a parent or an old family pattern, do one thing today that honors them anyway: a call, a memory shared, a prayer.' },
    ],
  },

  'Lech Lecha': {
    meaning: 'Go forth',
    summary: 'God tells Abram to leave his land and his father’s house for a land God will show him, promising that his family will become a great nation and a blessing to the world. Abram and Sarai travel to Canaan, go down to Egypt in a famine, part ways with Abram’s nephew Lot, and rescue Lot in a war. God makes a covenant with Abram, Hagar gives birth to Ishmael, and Abram and Sarai get new names: Abraham and Sarah.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Go, even without a map', text: '“Go from your land… to the land that I will show you.” Abram leaves without knowing where he’s going. Growing often means leaving what’s familiar before the destination is clear.', source: 'Genesis 12:1' },
      { title: 'Be a blessing', text: 'God promises Abram, “You shall be a blessing,” and that all the families of the earth will be blessed through him. Being chosen isn’t about being better. It’s a job: making other people’s lives better.', source: 'Genesis 12:2–3' },
      { title: 'Choose peace over winning', text: 'When their shepherds quarrel, Abram, the elder, lets Lot choose the land first: “Let there be no strife between you and me, for we are kinsmen.” Keeping the family together mattered more than getting the best land.', source: 'Genesis 13:8–9' },
      { title: 'Trust counts', text: 'Old and childless, Abram is told to look at the stars: his children will be as many. “He trusted in the LORD, and He counted it as righteousness.” Believing in a promise you can’t see yet is a kind of goodness.', source: 'Genesis 15:5–6' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 12:1', headline: 'Abraham’s faith began with a question about a burning palace.', text: 'Why Abram? The Torah never says what he did to be chosen. The Midrash fills the silence with a parable: a traveler sees a great building <i>doleket</i>, a word that means lit up or on fire, and asks, “Can it be that this building has no one in charge?” The owner looks out at him: “I am the owner of the building.” So Abram looked at the world and asked whether anyone was responsible for it, and God looked out and said, “I am the owner of the world” (Bereshit Rabbah 39:1). Then come the words “Go forth.”', try: 'Pick one thing burning in your world, at work, in your city, in your family, that everyone shrugs at. Ask today who is responsible for it, and whether part of the answer is you.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 13:3', headline: 'After Egypt, Abram retraces every step back to his first altar.', text: 'In Egypt Abram passed Sarai off as his sister, and Pharaoh himself had to rebuke him: “What is this you have done to me?” Abram leaves rich and says nothing. Then the Torah slows down: he goes back “by his journeys” to where his tent was “at first,” to the altar he built “at first.” Rashi says he stayed at the same inns as on the way down, and paid off the debts he had left behind (Bereshit Rabbah 41:3). After a chapter you’re not proud of, the way back is the same road, settling accounts as you go.', try: 'Go back to one person or promise from before a chapter you regret. Settle one debt today: money, an apology, or a thank-you.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 13:10', headline: 'Lot chose the view, and the view came with Sodom.', text: 'Offered first pick, Lot “lifted up his eyes” and saw the Jordan plain, well watered, “like the garden of the LORD, like the land of Egypt.” The Torah quietly adds: this was before God destroyed Sodom, and its people were very wicked. Rashi hears a pun in “Lot traveled <i>mikedem</i>,” eastward: he traveled away from the <i>Kadmon</i>, the Ancient One of the world, saying, “I want neither Abram nor his God” (Bereshit Rabbah 41:7). Four verses later God tells Abram to lift up his eyes too, toward a land he doesn’t own yet. Same gesture, two ways of seeing.', try: 'Before one choice today, a purchase, a job lead, a plan, ask not only how it looks but what kind of people and life come with it.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 14:14', headline: 'Abram wouldn’t fight over land, but went to war for Lot.', text: 'One chapter ago Abram gave up the best land rather than quarrel. Now four kings have carried off Lot, the nephew who left him for Sodom, and Abram arms his men, 318 of them, and chases the armies by night all the way to Dan. Rashi quotes the Talmud on that number: 318 is the numerical value of the name Eliezer, and the rabbis say his servant Eliezer alone went with him (Nedarim 32a). The man who wouldn’t fight for property risks everything, nearly alone, for a relative who chose against him.', try: 'Is there someone who chose against you but is now in real trouble? Do one concrete thing today to help, without first settling who was right.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 14:24', headline: 'Abram refused the spoils, but made sure his allies got theirs.', text: 'The king of Sodom offers Abram the loot. He refuses all of it, “not a thread or a sandal strap,” so no one can say, “I made Abram rich.” The Talmud says that for those words his children earned the blue thread of <i>tzitzit</i> and the strap of <i>tefillin</i> (Sotah 17a). But in the next breath: “only the share of the men who went with me, Aner, Eshkol and Mamre: let them take their share.” Rashi notes they only guarded the baggage, and still get paid. Your principles are yours to keep; don’t make others pay for them.', try: 'Wherever you hold yourself to a strict standard today, about money, food or work, check that you aren’t quietly charging someone else for it.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 16:13', headline: 'The first name for God came from the one they drove out.', text: 'Hagar, an Egyptian servant, has fled Sarai’s harshness and sits pregnant and alone by a desert spring. There she becomes the first person in the Torah to give God a name: <i>El-roi</i>, “the God who sees me,” and her son will be Ishmael, “God hears.” The Ramban doesn’t look away: “Our mother sinned” in mistreating her, and Abraham too, for letting it happen. Not Abraham in his tent but the woman pushed out of it named God first. Years later Isaac makes his home by her well; Rashi says he went there to bring her back.', try: 'Think of someone you, or people close to you, have been hard on or left out. Reach out to them today, the way Isaac went to Hagar’s well.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 17:23', headline: 'At ninety-nine, Abraham did it that same day, in broad daylight.', text: 'God commands circumcision for Abraham and every male in his household. Abraham is ninety-nine. The Torah says he did it “on that very day,” and repeats the phrase three verses later. Rashi explains: in daylight, not under cover of night, because he was afraid of neither the pagans nor the scoffers, so that no one could say, “If we had seen him, we would never have let him” (Bereshit Rabbah 47:9). He doesn’t wait for a quieter time or a safer place. A commitment made in hiding is easy to take back.', try: 'Take the commitment you’ve been planning to start “soon,” and start it today where at least one person can see.' },
    ],
  },

  'Vayera': {
    meaning: 'And He appeared',
    summary: 'God appears to Abraham as he welcomes three strangers, who announce that Sarah will have a son. Abraham argues with God to save the city of Sodom; it is destroyed, but his nephew Lot is rescued. Isaac is born, Hagar and Ishmael are sent away, and finally God tests Abraham by asking him to offer up Isaac, stopping him at the last moment.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Welcome the stranger', text: 'Still recovering from his circumcision, in the heat of the day, Abraham runs to greet three strangers and serves them a feast. The Talmud learns from here that welcoming guests is greater than receiving the Divine Presence.', source: 'Genesis 18:1–8 · Shabbat 127a' },
      { title: 'Argue for justice, even with God', text: '“Shall the Judge of all the earth not do justice?” Abraham bargains with God for Sodom, from fifty innocent people down to ten. Faith here isn’t silence. It’s caring enough to push back.', source: 'Genesis 18:23–32' },
      { title: 'Don’t look back', text: 'Lot’s wife looks back at burning Sodom and becomes a pillar of salt. Being rescued means walking forward; staring at what you’re losing can freeze you in place.', source: 'Genesis 19:26' },
      { title: '“Here I am”', text: 'Three times in the story of the binding of Isaac, Abraham answers <i>Hineni</i>, “here I am”: to God, to Isaac, and to the angel. Being fully there for each person who calls you is the heart of the story.', source: 'Genesis 22:1, 7, 11' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 18:13', headline: 'God edits Sarah’s words to keep the peace in her marriage.', text: 'Overhearing that she will have a son, Sarah laughs to herself: “Now that I’m worn out, will I have pleasure, and my lord is old?” God repeats it to Abraham with a change: “Why did Sarah laugh, saying, ‘Shall I really give birth, old as I am?’” The part about Abraham is gone. Rashi: Scripture altered her words for the sake of peace. The Talmud draws the lesson: “Great is peace, for even the Holy One departed from the truth for its sake” (Yevamot 65b). Not every true thing needs to be passed on.', try: 'Today, when you pass along what someone said, about a friend, a partner, a parent, leave out the part that would only wound.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 18:33', headline: 'The bargaining for Sodom ended when Abraham stopped, not God.', text: 'Abraham argues God down from fifty innocent people to forty-five, forty, thirty, twenty, ten. Then he goes quiet. “The LORD went away when He had finished speaking to Abraham, and Abraham returned to his place.” Rashi reads it like a courtroom: as soon as the defender had nothing more to say, the Judge left; the prosecutor kept on, and the two angels came to Sodom (Bereshit Rabbah 49:14). Rashi also says why he stopped at ten: eight righteous people in Noah’s ark hadn’t saved their world. Still, God never refused. Abraham stopped asking.', try: 'Think of a cause or a person you stopped speaking up for because it felt hopeless. Make one more call, letter or request on their behalf today.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 19:16', headline: 'With Sodom about to burn, Lot lingered over his property.', text: 'The angels warn Lot to flee; his sons-in-law think he’s joking. Dawn comes, the angels urge him again, “and he lingered.” Rashi says why: to save his property. So the angels seize him, his wife and his daughters by the hand, “in the LORD’s mercy on him,” and put him outside the city. This is the man who chose the plain of Sodom for its rich land. Even with fire about to fall, he can’t let go of what he came for. A few verses later, his wife looks back.', try: 'Name one thing, a job, an apartment, a possession, a relationship, that you’re staying for when you know you should go. Write down what it would take to leave.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 21:1', headline: 'Abraham prayed for another family’s children, then Sarah conceived.', text: 'The chapter before ends with Abraham, still childless with Sarah, praying for King Abimelech, whose household God has made barren, and they are healed. The very next verse: “The LORD remembered Sarah as He had said.” Rashi explains the order: whoever prays for someone else while needing the very same thing is answered first (Bava Kamma 92a). Abraham had expected the worst of this king, “surely there is no fear of God in this place,” and still prays for his family. The way out of your own waiting can run through someone else’s.', try: 'Pray, or work, today for someone who needs the very thing you’re still waiting for: a job, a partner, healing.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 21:17', headline: 'God saves Ishmael knowing what his descendants will do.', text: 'The water is gone. Hagar sets her son under a bush and sits a bowshot away: “Let me not watch the child die.” Then “God heard the voice of the boy, where he is.” Rashi, from the Midrash: the angels protest, “His descendants will one day kill Your children with thirst, and You bring up a well for him?” God asks: “What is he now, righteous or wicked?” “Righteous.” “By what he is now, I judge him” (Bereshit Rabbah 53:14). Even God refuses to punish a person for a future that hasn’t happened yet.', try: 'Think of someone you’re already bracing against for what they might do. Today, treat them only by what they’ve actually done.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 21:25', headline: 'A peace that skips the complaint isn’t peace.', text: 'Abimelech, king of Gerar, comes to make a treaty, and Abraham agrees to swear. But first “Abraham reproved Abimelech about the well of water that Abimelech’s servants had seized.” He doesn’t swallow it to keep things pleasant. The Midrash: Rabbi Yosei bar Chanina says rebuke leads to love, and “any love without rebuke is not love.” Reish Lakish adds, “Any peace without rebuke is not peace” (Bereshit Rabbah 54:3). Abimelech says he never knew; they make the covenant, and the place is named Beersheba, the well of the oath.', try: 'Raise one small thing you’ve been swallowing with someone close to you, calmly and specifically, so the peace between you is real.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 22:19', headline: 'Two walked up the mountain together; only one is said to return.', text: 'Twice on the way up, the Torah says, “the two of them walked on together.” Coming down: “Abraham returned to his young men.” Where is Isaac? The Midrash says Abraham sent him to study Torah with Shem (Bereshit Rabbah 56:11). Ibn Ezra insists Isaac was there, just not named, and says anyone who claims Abraham actually slaughtered him contradicts Scripture. But the silence is real: the Torah never again records father and son speaking to each other. Some ordeals you survive together and still can’t talk about.', try: 'Is there someone you went through something hard with and have barely talked to since? Break the silence today, even with one small sentence.' },
    ],
  },

  'Chayei Sara': {
    meaning: 'The life of Sarah',
    summary: 'Sarah dies at 127, and Abraham buys the Cave of Machpelah in Hebron to bury her: the first land the family owns in Canaan. He sends his servant to find a wife for Isaac. At a well, Rebecca’s kindness to the servant and his camels shows she’s the one. Isaac marries Rebecca and is comforted after his mother’s death. Abraham dies and is buried by his sons Isaac and Ishmael together.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Kindness is the real test', text: 'Abraham’s servant asks for a sign: the right woman will offer water to him and to his ten camels too. Rebecca does more than asked, running back and forth to the well. Character shows in what we do beyond the minimum.', source: 'Genesis 24:14–20' },
      { title: 'Honor the dead', text: 'Abraham insists on paying full price, in public, for a place to bury Sarah. Caring for the dead, who can never repay us, is what Jewish tradition calls <i>chesed shel emet</i>: true kindness.', source: 'Genesis 23:3–16' },
      { title: 'Love that heals', text: '“Isaac brought her into his mother Sarah’s tent… and he loved her, and Isaac was comforted after his mother.” The first time the Torah says a husband loved his wife, the love comes with healing from grief.', source: 'Genesis 24:67' },
      { title: 'Families can come back together', text: 'When Abraham dies, “Isaac and Ishmael his sons buried him.” The brothers, separated since childhood, stand together at their father’s grave. Rashi notes that Ishmael let Isaac go first: a sign he had changed.', source: 'Genesis 25:9 · Rashi' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 23:2', headline: 'Rashi says the binding of Isaac cost Sarah her life.', text: 'Sarah dies in Hebron, “and Abraham came to mourn for Sarah and to weep for her.” Came from where? From Beersheba, where he settled after Mount Moriah. Rashi explains why her death comes right after the binding of Isaac: when she heard that her son had been readied for slaughter and nearly slaughtered, “her soul flew from her,” and she died (from Pirkei deRabbi Eliezer 32). The Torah’s most famous test has a casualty the story never shows. Abraham passed the test; Sarah paid for it.', try: 'Think of someone who paid a price for a choice you made for good reasons. Say it to them today: “I know this cost you.”' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 24:7', headline: 'When Abraham started out, God was only the God of heaven.', text: 'Making his servant swear an oath, Abraham calls God “God of heaven and God of earth.” Four verses later, remembering the day God took him from his father’s house, he says only “God of heaven.” Rashi explains the difference: back then people didn’t acknowledge God, so He was God of heaven but not yet of the earth. Now, Rashi has Abraham say, “I have made His name familiar in people’s mouths” (Bereshit Rabbah 59:7). God becomes God of the earth when people make Him part of ordinary life. That was Abraham’s life’s work.', try: 'Bring holiness into one ordinary place today: a blessing before you eat, an honest deal where cutting corners is normal, a kindness nobody expects.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 24:14', headline: 'The Talmud calls the servant’s famous prayer a reckless one.', text: 'At the well, the servant asks God for a sign: the girl who says, “Drink, and I’ll water your camels too,” will be the one for Isaac. It’s a wise test: kindness to a stranger and to animals. But the Talmud lists him among three people who asked improperly: by that sign she might have been lame or blind, and he had already promised to bring her (Taanit 4a). He was answered well anyway, with Rebecca. And even then he stands “gazing at her in silence” to know if it’s real. Watch for signs; don’t hand them your judgment.', try: 'If you’re waiting for a sign about a decision, choose one real test of character to watch for today, and then use your own judgment too.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 24:39', headline: 'The servant found Isaac a wife while hoping for his own daughter.', text: 'Telling Rebecca’s family his story, the servant recalls asking Abraham, “Perhaps the woman will not follow me.” In the Torah scroll that “perhaps,” <i>ulai</i>, is spelled without one letter, so it can be read <i>eilai</i>, “to me.” Rashi: the servant had a daughter, and was hoping Abraham would turn to him for a match for Isaac. Abraham said no (Bereshit Rabbah 59:9). And still this man travels hundreds of miles, prays, and succeeds at the mission that ends his own hope. That’s faithfulness: doing the job well when you wanted a different outcome.', try: 'Do one task wholeheartedly today for someone else’s success, even if part of you wanted it for yourself.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 24:63', headline: 'The afternoon prayer began with a grieving man in a field.', text: 'Isaac goes out “to <i>suach</i> in the field toward evening.” Rashi says it means to pray, as in the psalm “a prayer of the afflicted, when he pours out his <i>siach</i>, his complaint.” The Talmud says this is where the afternoon prayer, <i>Minchah</i>, began (Berakhot 26b). Think of who is praying: the son who lay bound on the altar, now mourning his mother, alone outdoors as the day turns. Prayer starts as pouring out what weighs on you. And when he lifts his eyes, camels are coming: Rebecca.', try: 'Before dark today, take ten minutes outside alone and say out loud what’s actually weighing on you, to God or to the sky.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 25:11', headline: 'Abraham blessed the world, but was afraid to bless his son.', text: 'God told Abraham, “You shall be a blessing,” and gave him the power to bless whomever he chose. Yet he dies without blessing Isaac. Rashi explains: Abraham foresaw that Esau would come from Isaac, and feared his blessing would flow on to him. So he said, “Let the Master of blessings come and bless whomever He sees fit.” After Abraham’s death, “God blessed Isaac his son.” Rashi’s other reading: God came to comfort Isaac as mourners are comforted. Either way, a father held back, and God stepped in.', try: 'Bless someone today, a child, a friend, a younger colleague, out loud and specifically, without worrying about what they’ll do with it.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 25:17', headline: 'The Torah gives Ishmael the death of a righteous man.', text: 'The portion closes with Ishmael: twelve princes, as God promised Abraham, and a life of 137 years. Then “he expired and died, and was gathered to his people.” Rashi notes that “expired,” <i>vayigva</i>, together with dying, is used only of the righteous (Bava Batra 16b). They are the same words the Torah used for Abraham’s death a few verses earlier. The boy sent into the desert with a skin of water ends his story described in his father’s terms. The Torah doesn’t write off the son who wasn’t chosen.', try: 'Think of someone you wrote off years ago as the family’s problem. Find out how their life actually turned out, and tell them one good thing you see.' },
    ],
  },

  'Toldot': {
    meaning: 'Generations',
    summary: 'After twenty years of waiting, Rebecca gives birth to twins, Esau and Jacob, who struggle even before they’re born. Esau sells his birthright to Jacob for a bowl of stew. Isaac re-digs his father’s wells and makes peace with the Philistines. When Isaac, old and blind, plans to bless Esau, Rebecca and Jacob trick him into blessing Jacob instead, and Jacob has to flee his furious brother.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Different children, different paths', text: 'Esau is a hunter, a man of the field; Jacob is a quiet man who stays among the tents. Each parent loves a different son. “Teach a child according to his way” (Proverbs 22:6): each person needs their own path.', source: 'Genesis 25:27–28' },
      { title: 'Don’t trade the future for right now', text: 'Hungry from the field, Esau sells his birthright for a bowl of lentil stew: “I’m about to die anyway, so what good is a birthright to me?” What we want this minute can crowd out what matters most.', source: 'Genesis 25:29–34' },
      { title: 'Re-dig the old wells', text: 'Isaac reopens the wells his father Abraham dug, which the Philistines had filled in, and gives them the same names his father had. Sometimes progress means recovering what an earlier generation already found.', source: 'Genesis 26:18' },
      { title: 'Deception has a cost', text: 'Jacob gets the blessing by disguise, and he pays for it: years away from home, and later he is tricked himself. The Torah doesn’t hide its heroes’ mistakes. It shows how they echo.', source: 'Genesis 27' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 25:22', headline: 'After twenty years of waiting, Rebecca wished she had never existed.', text: 'Isaac and Rebecca wait twenty years for a child. When twins finally come, they struggle inside her, and she says, <i>lamah zeh anochi</i>. Rashi reads it as “why did I long for this?” Ramban reads it darker: “If it is to be like this, why am I in the world? Would that I did not exist.” Then, instead of only despairing, she goes “to inquire of the LORD” herself. The answer, two nations, the elder serving the younger, doesn’t take the pain away. It gives it meaning, and the Torah never says she told Isaac.', try: 'Take the question that’s been sitting heaviest on you and bring it somewhere real today: in prayer, to a rabbi or a therapist, or written out in full.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 26:12', headline: 'Isaac sowed in a famine and counted his harvest for charity.', text: 'There is a famine, and Isaac stays in Gerar, frightened enough to pass Rebecca off as his sister, as his father once did. Then: “Isaac sowed in that land and reaped in that year a hundredfold.” Why both “that land” and “that year”? Rashi: the soil was hard and the year was hard. And the hundredfold was measured against an estimate, which the rabbis say he made in order to tithe (Bereshit Rabbah 64:6). In the worst year, frightened, in a king’s land not his own, he was counting what he could give.', try: 'In whatever feels like your famine year, set aside a specific amount of money or time to give away this week, and give part of it today.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 26:22', headline: 'Ramban reads Isaac’s three wells as the three Temples.', text: 'Why does the Torah spend so long on wells? Ramban says it hides a story about the future. Isaac’s first well is fought over, and he names it <i>Esek</i>, “contention”: the First Temple, which the nations fought over until they destroyed it. The second, <i>Sitnah</i>, “hostility”: the Second Temple, opposed from its first days until it fell. The third no one fights over: <i>Rehovot</i>, “wide spaces,” the Temple still to come, built without quarrel. Isaac’s answer to losing two wells isn’t to fight. It’s to dig again.', try: 'Think of something you built that was lost or fought over. Instead of rehashing the fight, take one step today toward digging the next one.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 26:29', headline: 'Abimelech rewrote the expulsion as a peaceful farewell.', text: 'Abimelech had told Isaac, “Go away from us,” and his people had stopped up the wells. Now he arrives wanting a treaty. Isaac asks: “Why have you come to me, when you hate me and sent me away?” Abimelech rewrites the history: “We did not touch you, we did only good to you, and sent you away in peace.” Rashi explains “we did not touch you”: that was when we said, “Go from us.” Isaac doesn’t argue. He makes them a feast, and they part in peace. He said the truth once, then let them keep their face.', try: 'With someone who remembers your shared history differently, say your truth once, plainly, then let them keep their version and keep the peace.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 27:13', headline: 'Rebecca took her son’s curse upon herself.', text: 'Jacob balks at deceiving his father: “I’ll bring a curse on myself, not a blessing.” Rebecca answers, “Your curse be upon me, my son; just listen to me.” Onkelos’s Aramaic translation adds what she knew: “It was told to me in prophecy that the curses will not come upon you.” And she pays. She sends Jacob away “for a few days,” and the Torah never shows them together again. Rashi says her death was kept quiet so people wouldn’t curse the mother of Esau, and the Torah never openly reports it.', try: 'If you’re pushing someone toward something you believe in, ask yourself honestly today what price you’re willing to pay for it yourself.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 27:34', headline: 'Esau’s bitter cry comes back, generations later, to Jacob’s children.', text: 'When Esau learns the blessing is gone, “he cried out a great and very bitter cry: Bless me too, my father!” The Midrash hears those words again centuries later, in the book of Esther. When Mordechai learns of Haman’s decree against the Jews, he “cried out a great and bitter cry.” Rabbi Chanina: don’t think God is lax; He is patient, and He collects. Jacob made Esau cry out once, and he was answered for it in Shushan (Bereshit Rabbah 67:4). The Torah’s hero doesn’t get a pass on his brother’s tears.', try: 'Think of someone who lost something because you won. Do one thing today that acknowledges their loss, even if you’d make the same choice again.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 28:9', headline: 'Esau tried to change by adding a wife, not by letting go.', text: 'Esau notices that his Canaanite wives grieve his parents, so he goes to Ishmael’s family and marries Mahalat, “in addition to his wives.” The Midrash is torn. Rabbi Yehoshua ben Levi says Esau was considering repentance, and the Midrash reads her name, Mahalat, as “forgiven.” But Rabbi Elazar points to those words, “in addition to his wives”: had he divorced the first ones, it would have been right; instead it was “pain upon pain” (Bereshit Rabbah 67:13). Real change usually means giving something up, not just adding something good on top.', try: 'Pick one change you’ve been attempting by adding something new, and ask what you’d have to stop doing for it to work. Stop it today.' },
    ],
  },

  'Vayetzei': {
    meaning: 'And he went out',
    summary: 'Running from Esau, Jacob dreams of a ladder reaching to heaven with angels going up and down it, and God promises to protect him. In Haran he falls in love with Rachel and works seven years for her, but her father Laban tricks him into marrying her sister Leah first. Jacob marries both, and over twenty years his family grows to eleven sons and a daughter. Finally he leaves with his family and flocks, and makes peace with Laban.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'God was here, and I didn’t know it', text: 'Waking from his dream of the ladder, Jacob says, “Surely the LORD is in this place, and I did not know it!” Holiness can turn up anywhere, even on a rock you’re sleeping on while running from home.', source: 'Genesis 28:16' },
      { title: 'Love makes time fly', text: 'Jacob works seven years for Rachel, “and they seemed to him like a few days, because of his love for her.” What we do out of love doesn’t feel like a burden.', source: 'Genesis 29:20' },
      { title: 'Saying thank you', text: 'When her fourth son is born, Leah names him Judah: “This time I will thank the LORD.” The Talmud says no one had thanked God like this before Leah. Jews, <i>Yehudim</i>, are named for Judah: people of thanks.', source: 'Genesis 29:35 · Berakhot 7b' },
      { title: 'Honest work', text: 'Jacob tells Laban: “By day the heat consumed me, and the frost by night, and sleep fled from my eyes.” He worked faithfully for a boss who cheated him. Maimonides holds him up as the model of how to do a job.', source: 'Genesis 31:40 · Rambam, Laws of Hiring 13:7' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 28:20', headline: 'Jacob’s “if” was doubt about himself, not about God.', text: 'At the top of the ladder God promises Jacob everything: the land, countless children, “I am with you, and I will guard you wherever you go.” Jacob wakes and vows: “If God will be with me and guard me, and give me bread to eat and clothes to wear… then the LORD will be my God.” Why “if,” after such a promise? Ramban quotes the Midrash: “there is no assurance for the righteous in this world” (Bereshit Rabbah 76:2), because sin can undo a promise. He trusts God; it’s himself he isn’t sure of.', try: 'Write down one good thing in your life you’re afraid you could ruin through your own fault, and put one concrete safeguard around it today.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 29:17', headline: 'Leah prayed her eyes weak to escape the husband everyone assigned her.', text: 'The Torah introduces the sisters bluntly: “Leah’s eyes were weak, and Rachel was beautiful.” The Talmud refuses to read it as an insult. People used to say: Rebecca has two sons and Laban two daughters, the elder for the elder. So Leah sat at the crossroads asking travelers about Esau: “A wicked man; he robs people.” And about Jacob: “a quiet man, dwelling in tents.” She cried and prayed until her eyelashes fell out (Bava Batra 123a; Rashi). She asked questions, she prayed, and the life everyone had planned for her never happened.', try: 'Name one future that people around you assume for you. Today, ask one hard question about it and decide for yourself whether you want it.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 29:25', headline: 'The morning after, Leah reminded Jacob who taught her to deceive.', text: 'Jacob works seven years for Rachel, and in the morning, “behold, it was Leah.” He storms at Laban: “Why did you deceive me?” The Midrash imagines the conversation with Leah herself. Jacob: “You’re a deceiver, daughter of a deceiver! All night I called you Rachel and you answered me.” Leah: “Is there a master without disciples? Didn’t your father call you Esau, and you answered him?” (Bereshit Rabbah 70:19). The man who fooled his blind father under a borrowed name wakes up fooled the same way, and the Torah lets him hear it.', try: 'Think of the thing that most enrages you when others do it to you. Check honestly today whether you’ve ever done a version of it yourself.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 30:22', headline: 'God remembered Rachel for the night she kept silent.', text: 'Rachel has begged Jacob, “Give me children, or I am dead,” given him her maid, bargained for mandrakes. Nothing works. Then “God remembered Rachel.” Remembered what? Rashi: that she gave her sister the secret signs. The Talmud tells it: Jacob had arranged signs with Rachel so Laban couldn’t switch the brides. When Rachel saw Leah being led in, she thought, “Now my sister will be shamed,” and gave her the signs (Megillah 13b). What God remembered wasn’t her pleading or her schemes, but the night she quietly gave away her own wedding.', try: 'Do one generous thing today that costs you something and that no one will ever find out about.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 31:14', headline: 'Rachel and Leah finally agree, about what their father did.', text: 'For years Rachel and Leah have competed for Jacob’s love, even naming their sons after the rivalry. Now Jacob calls them out to the field and tells them God has said to go home. For the first time, the sisters answer with one voice: “Do we still have any share in our father’s house? He treats us as strangers. He sold us, and he has used up our money.” Rashi: what inheritance could we hope for, alongside his sons? What finally unites them is saying the truth about their father out loud. “Whatever God has told you, do it.”', try: 'With a sibling or someone you’ve competed with, name one hard truth you both know about where you came from, and say it to each other today.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 31:32', headline: 'Jacob unknowingly cursed the woman he loved most.', text: 'Laban catches up with Jacob’s family and accuses them of stealing his household gods. Jacob, sure of his people, swears: “Anyone with whom you find your gods shall not live.” He doesn’t know that Rachel took them; Rashi says she did it to wean her father from idol worship. Laban searches her tent; she sits on them and isn’t caught. But Rashi says that because of Jacob’s curse, Rachel died on the road (Bereshit Rabbah 74:9). A few confident words, from a man who thought he knew everything in his own house.', try: 'Before you make a sweeping promise or accusation today (“never,” “anyone who”), stop and ask what you might not know.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 31:52', headline: 'Jacob and Laban kept the peace with a pile of stones.', text: 'After twenty years of cheating and a tense chase, Jacob and Laban don’t reconcile; they draw a border. They heap up stones, and each names the heap in his own language, Laban in Aramaic, Jacob in Hebrew: “heap of witness.” “I will not cross this heap to you, and you will not cross it to me, for harm.” Rashi: for harm you may not cross, but for trade you may (Bereshit Rabbah 74:16). Then Laban kisses his daughters and grandchildren and goes home. Not every relationship can be healed. Some can be kept safe with a clear line.', try: 'Where a relationship keeps hurting you, set one clear boundary today, and say plainly what is still welcome across it.' },
    ],
  },

  'Vayishlach': {
    meaning: 'And he sent',
    summary: 'Jacob prepares to meet Esau after twenty years, sending gifts and praying for help. The night before, he wrestles with a mysterious man until dawn and is given a new name: Israel. The brothers meet and embrace in tears. Jacob’s daughter Dinah is assaulted in Shechem, and her brothers take violent revenge. Rachel dies giving birth to Benjamin, and Isaac dies, buried by both of his sons.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Prepare every way you can', text: 'Facing Esau, Jacob sends gifts, prays, and gets his camp ready to defend itself. Rashi says he prepared three ways: with a gift, with prayer, and for battle. Wise people don’t rely on just one approach.', source: 'Genesis 32:4–22 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Wrestle with it', text: 'Jacob wrestles all night and won’t let go until he’s blessed. His new name, Israel, means one who “struggled with God and with people, and won.” Struggle isn’t the opposite of faith. For Israel, it’s the name.', source: 'Genesis 32:25–29' },
      { title: '“I am too small”', text: 'Before asking God for anything, Jacob prays: “I am too small for all the kindness and faithfulness You have shown me.” Humility and gratitude come first.', source: 'Genesis 32:11' },
      { title: 'People can make up', text: 'Esau, who once swore to kill Jacob, runs to meet him, hugs him, kisses him, and they both cry. Twenty years of anger end in an embrace. No rift is too old to heal.', source: 'Genesis 33:4' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 32:8', headline: 'Jacob was as afraid of killing as of being killed.', text: 'The messengers return: Esau is coming to meet Jacob, with four hundred men. “Jacob was greatly afraid, and distressed.” Why two words? Rashi, from the Midrash: afraid that he might be killed, and distressed that he might have to kill (Bereshit Rabbah 76:2). Facing a brother who once planned to murder him, with every right to defend himself, Jacob is still troubled by the blood he might spill. A decent person counts the cost of winning, not only of losing. Then he divides his camp, prays, and sends gifts.', try: 'Before a conflict you expect today, write down not just what you’re afraid of losing, but what winning badly could cost the other person.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 32:29', headline: 'Jacob fought all night for a blessing he wouldn’t have to steal.', text: 'A man wrestles Jacob until dawn, and Jacob won’t let go: “not unless you bless me.” Rashi says what he really wants: admit that the blessings my father gave me, the ones Esau claims, are mine. The answer is a new name: “No longer Jacob, but Israel.” Rashi: no longer will it be said that the blessings came to you by <i>akvah</i>, trickery, the root of “Jacob,” but by nobility and in the open. Decades after taking a blessing in disguise, he finally receives one face to face, as himself, and it costs him a limp.', try: 'Is there something good in your life you got in a way you’re not proud of? Do one thing today that makes it honestly yours: a confession, a repayment, a thank-you.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 32:33', headline: 'Jewish law keeps Jacob’s wound on the table forever.', text: 'The sun rises as Jacob limps away; Rashi quotes the Midrash that it rose for his sake, to heal him. Then the Torah turns the injury into law: “Therefore the children of Israel do not eat the thigh sinew, to this day.” The Sefer HaChinuch explains why: it hints that Jacob’s children will suffer much in exile, at the hands of Esau’s descendants and others, but will not be destroyed, and their redeemer will come, as the dawn came for Jacob. Every kosher kitchen still remembers a wound, and a sunrise.', try: 'Think of an old wound that shaped you. Instead of hiding it today, tell one person what it taught you, and what healed it.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 33:18', headline: 'After Laban and Esau, Jacob arrives whole.', text: 'Jacob has been cheated for twenty years, has wrestled until dawn, limped, and handed Esau hundreds of animals. Then: “Jacob arrived <i>shalem</i>, whole, at the city of Shechem.” Rashi, from the Talmud, counts three kinds of whole: whole in body, because his limp was healed; whole in his possessions, missing nothing despite the huge gift; and whole in his Torah, because in Laban’s house he hadn’t forgotten what he had learned (Shabbat 33b). The test of twenty hard years isn’t what you gained. It’s what you didn’t lose.', try: 'Take stock tonight of what stayed whole through your hardest years, in body, in livelihood, in what you believe. Thank God, out loud, for one of each.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 34:30', headline: 'Maimonides defends the massacre at Shechem; Ramban won’t.', text: 'After Shechem rapes Dinah, her brothers trick the town into circumcising itself, then Simeon and Levi kill every man. Was it justice? Maimonides says yes: every people must bring wrongdoers to judgment, and the townsmen saw and did nothing, so they deserved death (Laws of Kings 9:14). Ramban disagrees: they had no power to judge their own prince, and they had just agreed to become one people with Jacob’s family. Jacob protests, “You have brought trouble on me,” and on his deathbed curses their anger. Their answer hangs unanswered: “Should he treat our sister like a harlot?”', try: 'The next time you feel righteous anger, ask two questions before you act: what was done to us, and what will my response do to people who didn’t do it?' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 35:22', headline: 'The Talmud says Reuben’s “sin” was moving a bed for his mother.', text: 'The Torah says it flatly: “Reuben went and lay with Bilhah, his father’s concubine, and Israel heard.” The Talmud objects: “Whoever says Reuben sinned is simply mistaken” (Shabbat 55b). Rashi tells what happened: after Rachel died, Jacob moved his bed into the tent of Bilhah, Rachel’s maid. Reuben, hurt for his mother Leah, said, “My mother’s sister was her rival; must her sister’s maid be one too?” and moved the bed. For disturbing his father’s bed, Scripture writes it as if he had lain with her. Defending your mother can still cross a line.', try: 'If you’re tempted today to step into someone else’s relationship on another person’s behalf, ask first whether it’s really your bed to move.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 36:22', headline: 'Amalek came from a woman the patriarchs refused to accept.', text: 'In a dry list of Edomite chiefs comes a half-verse: “and Lotan’s sister was Timna.” Why is it there? The Talmud tells her story. Timna, a princess, wanted to convert. She came to Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, and they would not accept her. So she became a concubine of Eliphaz, Esau’s son: better a servant in this nation than a noblewoman in another. Her son was Amalek, who would torment Israel for generations. Why? “Because they should not have rejected her” (Sanhedrin 99b). Turning someone away can cost more than letting them in.', try: 'Who is knocking at your door, wanting in, a newcomer, a younger person, someone awkward or eager? Let one of them in today.' },
    ],
  },

  'Vayeshev': {
    meaning: 'And he settled',
    summary: 'Jacob gives his favorite son Joseph a special coat, and Joseph’s dreams of ruling over his brothers make them hate him. They throw him into a pit and sell him into slavery, then tell Jacob a wild animal killed him. Judah learns a hard lesson from his daughter-in-law Tamar. In Egypt, Joseph rises in Potiphar’s house, refuses Potiphar’s wife, and is thrown into prison, where he explains the dreams of Pharaoh’s butler and baker.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Favoritism hurts', text: 'Jacob loves Joseph most and gives him a fancy coat, and his brothers “could not speak a kind word to him.” The Talmud warns parents never to single out one child: a little fine wool led to slavery in Egypt.', source: 'Genesis 37:3–4 · Shabbat 10b' },
      { title: 'Own your mistakes', text: 'When Tamar shows that Judah is the father of her child, he says, “She is more right than I.” Admitting he was wrong, in public, is what makes Judah a leader.', source: 'Genesis 38:26' },
      { title: 'Say no, even unwatched', text: 'Potiphar’s wife pressures Joseph day after day. He refuses: “How could I do this great wrong and sin against God?” Integrity is who you are when it would be easy not to be.', source: 'Genesis 39:9–10' },
      { title: 'God is with you in the pit', text: 'As a slave and as a prisoner, “the LORD was with Joseph.” His circumstances keep falling apart; his character keeps lifting him up. Where you are isn’t who you are.', source: 'Genesis 39:2, 21' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 37:2', headline: 'Joseph’s troubles began with the reports he carried to his father.', text: 'At seventeen, Joseph shepherds with his brothers, “and Joseph brought bad reports of them to their father.” Rashi lists them: that they ate meat cut from living animals, called the maidservants’ sons slaves, and were suspected of immorality. Then he shows each one coming back on Joseph: the brothers slaughter a goat, properly, to stain his coat; he is sold as a slave; and his master’s wife pursues him (Bereshit Rabbah 84:7). The coat and the dreams didn’t start the hatred alone. A boy who informed on his brothers paid for every word.', try: 'Before you report someone’s failings today, to a boss, a parent, a friend, ask whether you’d say it to their face, and whether you’re telling it to help or to win.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 37:21', headline: 'Reuben saved Joseph halfway, and the Midrash wishes he’d gone further.', text: 'The brothers plan to kill Joseph. “Reuben heard, and saved him from their hands”: throw him into a pit instead, he says, meaning to come back and return him to his father. Reuben goes off; when he returns, the pit is empty. The Midrash says: had Reuben known that God would write of him, “Reuben heard and saved him,” he would have picked Joseph up and carried him to his father. Rabbi Yitzchak’s lesson: when you do a good deed, do it with a joyful heart (Vayikra Rabbah 34:8). Half a rescue left Joseph in a pit.', try: 'Take one good thing you’ve started for someone, a favor, a recommendation, a check-in, and carry it all the way to the finish today.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 37:35', headline: 'Jacob couldn’t be comforted, because his son wasn’t really dead.', text: 'The brothers bring Jacob the coat dipped in goat’s blood: “Please recognize it.” He says, “A wild beast has devoured him.” All his sons and daughters come to comfort him, “and he refused to be comforted.” Rashi: there is a decree that the dead are eventually forgotten from the heart, but not the living (Bereshit Rabbah 84:20). Jacob’s grief won’t heal because, though he doesn’t know it, Joseph is alive. Some losses don’t ease with time because they aren’t really over: the estranged brother, the friend who simply stopped calling.', try: 'If someone alive is missing from your life and it still aches, take one step today: toward them with a message, or toward accepting it, out loud.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 38:25', headline: 'Tamar used the words Judah once used to break his father.', text: 'Condemned to burn, Tamar sends Judah his own seal, cord and staff: “Please recognize, <i>haker na</i>, whose these are.” Those are the exact words the brothers used when they showed Jacob the bloody coat. The Midrash hears God speaking: “You said to your father, ‘Please recognize.’ By your life, Tamar will say to you, ‘Please recognize’” (Bereshit Rabbah 85:11). And she says it privately, without naming him; the Talmud learns that it’s better to be thrown into a furnace than to shame someone in public (Sotah 10b). Judah recognizes, and confesses.', try: 'If you have to confront someone today with something they did, do it the way Tamar did: privately, with the facts, leaving them room to admit it.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 39:6', headline: 'While his father mourned, Joseph curled his hair.', text: 'Sold as a slave, Joseph rises fast: Potiphar puts everything in his hands. Then the Torah adds, “Joseph was handsome and good-looking.” Rashi, from the Midrash Tanchuma: as soon as he saw himself in charge, he began to eat and drink and curl his hair. God said, “Your father is mourning, and you curl your hair? I will set the bear on you.” And in the very next verse, his master’s wife casts her eyes on him. Joseph the righteous was first a vain young man, comfortable too quickly while his father wept.', try: 'When something is going well for you, think of who in your life is still grieving or struggling, and reach out to them before you celebrate.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 39:11', headline: 'Joseph almost gave in, until he saw his father’s face.', text: '“One day Joseph came into the house to do his work, and none of the household was there.” What work? Rashi brings a dispute between Rav and Shmuel: one says his actual work; the other says he came intending to sin with her. Then, the Talmud says, his father’s image appeared to him in the window: your brothers’ names will be engraved on the High Priest’s stones; do you want yours erased? (Sotah 36b). The Torah’s model of self-control was not above temptation. He was held by the memory of who he came from.', try: 'Before a choice you might regret today, picture the face of the person whose respect you most want to keep, and ask what they would see.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 40:23', headline: 'Rashi says Joseph’s plea to the butler cost him two more years.', text: 'Joseph reads the butler’s dream rightly and begs him: “Remember me when things go well for you, and mention me to Pharaoh.” The portion ends: “The chief butler did not remember Joseph, and forgot him.” Rashi is hard on Joseph: because he put his trust in the butler, he had to stay in prison two more years, as the psalm says, “Happy is the man who makes the LORD his trust” (Bereshit Rabbah 89:3). Asking for help wasn’t the problem. Hanging all his hope on one forgetful man was.', try: 'Ask for the help you need today, then deliberately let go of whether that person comes through. Put your hope somewhere bigger.' },
    ],
  },

  'Miketz': {
    meaning: 'At the end',
    summary: 'Two years later, Pharaoh dreams of seven fat cows eaten by seven thin ones. Joseph, brought up from prison, explains: seven years of plenty are coming, then seven of famine. Pharaoh puts him in charge of all Egypt. When the famine comes, Joseph’s brothers come to buy food. He recognizes them, but they don’t recognize him, and he tests them, finally framing Benjamin with a stolen silver cup.',
    lessons: [
      { title: '“Not me. God.”', text: 'Asked to explain Pharaoh’s dream, Joseph answers, “Not I; God will give Pharaoh an answer.” Talent is real, but humble people know where it comes from.', source: 'Genesis 41:16' },
      { title: 'Save in the good years', text: 'Joseph’s plan is simple: store food during the seven good years to get through the seven bad ones. Wisdom is preparing for hard times while times are still good.', source: 'Genesis 41:33–36' },
      { title: 'Guilt can lead to change', text: 'Accused of spying, the brothers say to each other: “We are being punished because of our brother. We saw his distress when he pleaded with us, and we wouldn’t listen.” More than twenty years later, they finally say it out loud.', source: 'Genesis 42:21' },
      { title: 'Taking responsibility', text: 'Judah tells his father, “I will be responsible for him; you can hold me accountable.” The brother who once sold Joseph now puts himself on the line for Rachel’s other son, Benjamin.', source: 'Genesis 43:9' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 41:1', headline: 'The Midrash says God sets an end to every darkness.', text: '“At the end of two full years, Pharaoh dreamed.” Joseph has been a slave and a prisoner for about thirteen years, two of them since the butler forgot him. The Midrash opens with a verse from Job: “He sets an end to darkness.” God set a time for how long the world would be in darkness, and a time for how long Joseph would sit in the dark of prison; “when the end came, Pharaoh dreamed a dream” (Bereshit Rabbah 89:1). Joseph doesn’t engineer his release. The dream comes on schedule, and he is ready for it.', try: 'In whatever feels endless right now, pick one thing to do today that would make you ready on the day it ends.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 41:33', headline: 'Joseph gave God the credit, then pitched himself for the job.', text: 'Brought up from prison, Joseph tells Pharaoh, “Not I; God will answer.” He interprets the dreams, then keeps going, unasked: let Pharaoh find a man “discerning and wise” to run the land. Ramban explains each word: discerning, to ration food for every family and sell the surplus abroad; wise, to know how to keep stored grain from rotting. And Joseph laid it all out, Ramban says, “so that they would choose him for this task.” Humility about where your gifts come from doesn’t mean hiding them. Pharaoh answers: “Could we find another like him?”', try: 'Name one thing you’re genuinely good at that someone near you needs. Today, offer it plainly, without false modesty and without taking all the credit.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 41:51', headline: 'Joseph named his first son for forgetting his father’s house.', text: 'Joseph is thirty, ruler of Egypt, married to a priest’s daughter. He names his first son Manasseh: “God has made me forget all my hardship, and all my father’s house.” For twenty-two years he sends no word home. Ramban asks it bluntly: Egypt is about six days from Hebron; how could Joseph not send a single letter to comfort his father? He answers that Joseph was waiting for his dreams to come true; without that, Ramban says, his silence toward his father would be “a great sin.” Even so, the name stands. Success can be built on forgetting.', try: 'Is there someone from home you’ve let fade as life got better? Reach back to them today, before you have a reason to.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 42:8', headline: 'Joseph recognized them as brothers; they never recognized him as one.', text: 'More than twenty years later, the brothers bow before the governor of Egypt. “Joseph recognized his brothers, but they did not recognize him.” Rashi gives the plain reason: they were bearded when he left, and he was a beardless boy who now has a beard. Then he brings the Midrash: when they fell into his hands, he recognized them as his brothers and had mercy on them; when he fell into theirs, they did not recognize him as a brother (Bereshit Rabbah 91:7). Recognizing someone isn’t only seeing their face. It’s remembering, when you have the power, that they’re family.', try: 'Think of someone who depends on you right now, an employee, a younger relative, someone in your debt. Treat them today the way you’d want to be treated in their place.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 42:37', headline: 'Reuben offered his sons’ lives; Judah offered his own.', text: 'Simeon is held in Egypt, and the viceroy demands Benjamin. Jacob refuses. Reuben makes a dramatic offer: “Kill my two sons if I don’t bring him back to you.” Rashi, from the Midrash: Jacob would not accept it, saying, “What a foolish firstborn! Kill his sons? Are they not my sons too?” (Bereshit Rabbah 91:9). Then the food runs out, and Judah speaks: “I will be surety for him; from my hand you may demand him.” Jacob lets Benjamin go. A promise that puts someone else at risk isn’t responsibility. Putting yourself on the line is.', try: 'The next time you make a promise today, check what it would actually cost you, not someone else, if you failed to keep it.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 43:23', headline: 'The brothers who sold a slave now feared becoming slaves.', text: 'Brought into the viceroy’s house for a meal, the brothers panic: it’s about the money in our sacks, they say, “to fall upon us and take us as slaves.” Rashi notes that ordinary grain-buyers slept at inns, not in the governor’s house. Over twenty years after selling their brother into slavery, they can only read an invitation as a trap. The steward answers: “Peace to you, don’t be afraid. Your God, and the God of your father, put treasure in your sacks.” Rashi: your God, for your own merit; and if that isn’t enough, your father’s God, for his.', try: 'The next time someone is kind to you today and you suspect a catch, take it at face value once, and simply say thank you.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 43:30', headline: 'Benjamin named all ten of his sons after the brother he lost.', text: 'Seeing Benjamin, Joseph’s “compassion was stirred,” and he rushes out to weep. What stirred it? Rashi, from the Talmud: Joseph asks if he has a brother. “I had one, and I don’t know where he is.” Sons? Ten. Their names? Each one remembers Joseph: Bela, because he was swallowed up among the nations; Gera, because he lives as a stranger; Huppim, because I wasn’t at his wedding, and he wasn’t at mine (Sotah 36b). For years, every time Benjamin called one of his children, he was calling his brother.', try: 'Do one thing today that keeps someone you’ve lost present: say their name, tell a story about them, or reach out to their family.' },
    ],
  },

  'Vayigash': {
    meaning: 'And he came close',
    summary: 'Judah steps forward and offers to stay as a slave in Benjamin’s place. Unable to hold back any longer, Joseph reveals himself: “I am Joseph.” He forgives his brothers and sends for his father. Jacob, told that Joseph is alive, comes down to Egypt with the whole family, seventy people. Father and son are reunited, and the family settles in Goshen while Joseph runs Egypt through the famine.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Step forward', text: 'Judah approaches the most powerful man in Egypt and offers himself instead of Benjamin. Real repentance, Maimonides says, is facing the same situation again and choosing differently. That’s exactly what Judah does.', source: 'Genesis 44:18–34 · Rambam, Laws of Repentance 2:1' },
      { title: 'Find a purpose in the past', text: 'Joseph tells his brothers: “Don’t be upset that you sold me here, for God sent me ahead of you to save lives.” He doesn’t erase what they did, but he finds a purpose in it.', source: 'Genesis 45:5' },
      { title: 'Don’t fight on the way', text: 'Sending his brothers home, Joseph says, “Don’t quarrel on the way.” Families slip back into old blame easily. Peace has to be protected, especially right after it’s made.', source: 'Genesis 45:24' },
      { title: 'The heart needs time', text: 'Told that Joseph is alive, Jacob’s “heart went numb, for he did not believe them.” Only when he sees the wagons Joseph sent does his spirit come back to life. Good news can take time to sink in.', source: 'Genesis 45:26–27' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 44:18', headline: 'Judah stepped forward ready to fight, to plead, and to pray.', text: '<i>Vayigash</i>, “and Judah came close.” Came close for what? The Midrash argues: Rabbi Yehuda says for battle, Rabbi Nechemiah for conciliation, the Rabbis for prayer, each with a verse where the same word appears. Rabbi Elazar puts them together: if it’s war, I’m coming; if peace, I’m coming; if prayer, I’m coming (Bereshit Rabbah 93:6). And “please, my lord” is read as “take me”: to draw water, to serve, to chop wood, me and not him. Judah walks in prepared for anything except leaving his brother behind.', try: 'Walk into one hard conversation today prepared three ways: ready to stand firm, ready to make peace, and having asked for help first.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 45:3', headline: '“I am Joseph” left his brothers with nothing to say.', text: '“I am Joseph. Is my father still alive?” His brothers can’t answer him, “for they were terrified before him.” No list of charges, no speech about the pit; just his name. Abba Kohen Bardela says: “Woe to us from the day of judgment; woe to us from the day of rebuke.” Joseph was the youngest of the tribes, and they couldn’t stand up to his rebuke; when God comes to rebuke each person according to what he is, how much more so (Bereshit Rabbah 93:10). The hardest judgment isn’t a sentence. It’s being recognized.', try: 'Picture the person you’ve wronged most saying only your name to you. Do today what you’d want to have done before that moment.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 45:14', headline: 'At their reunion, Joseph and Benjamin wept for each other’s ruins.', text: 'After twenty-two years apart, Joseph “fell on his brother Benjamin’s neck and wept, and Benjamin wept on his neck.” Rashi, from the Midrash, says what they were crying for. Joseph wept for the two Temples that would stand in Benjamin’s land and be destroyed; Benjamin wept for the Tabernacle at Shiloh, in Joseph’s land, which would be destroyed too (Bereshit Rabbah 93:12). In the happiest moment of their lives, each brother cries not for his own loss but for his brother’s. That is what love looks like with nothing left between you.', try: 'Ask someone close to you what they’re most afraid of losing. Let yourself care about it today as if it were yours.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 45:27', headline: 'The proof that Joseph was alive was their last lesson together.', text: '“Joseph is still alive,” his sons tell Jacob, and his heart goes numb; he doesn’t believe them. Then he sees the wagons Joseph sent, and his spirit revives. Rashi: the wagons were a sign. The last law Joseph had studied with his father was the <i>eglah arufah</i>, the heifer brought when a body is found in the open field, and “wagons,” <i>agalot</i>, recalls <i>eglah</i>. The Mishnah says that law asks whether a town let a traveler leave without food or escort (Sotah 9:6). Years before, Jacob sent Joseph on the road to his brothers, and he never came back.', try: 'When someone you love sets out today, on a trip, a hard day, a new job, don’t just send them off. Walk them partway, or call to make sure they arrived.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 46:4', headline: 'God tells Jacob: I am going into exile with you.', text: 'At Beersheba, on the edge of the land, old Jacob is afraid to leave. God speaks to him at night: “Do not be afraid to go down to Egypt… I will go down with you to Egypt, and I will surely bring you up again.” Rabbi Shimon bar Yochai sees a pattern that runs through Jewish history: “Wherever Israel was exiled, the Divine Presence went with them,” to Egypt, to Babylon, and it will return with them (Megillah 29a). God doesn’t promise to keep Jacob out of exile. He promises to go into it with him.', try: 'Is someone you know in their own “Egypt” right now? Go down with them today: sit with them in it, without trying to fix it.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 47:9', headline: 'Jacob told Pharaoh his years had been few and bad.', text: 'Pharaoh meets Joseph’s father and asks a strange question: “How many are the days of the years of your life?” Jacob answers: 130; “few and bad have been the days of the years of my life.” Ramban admits he can’t see why: is it proper to complain to a king? His answer: Jacob had gone gray and looked ancient, and Pharaoh marveled. Jacob explains that his years are few beside his fathers’, but hard, and the toil and groaning have aged him. Then the refugee blesses the king. Suffering shows on a face.', try: 'Look closely today at someone who seems worn out, older than their years. Ask them about it, gently, and listen to the answer.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 47:21', headline: 'Joseph moved all Egypt around so his brothers wouldn’t stand out.', text: 'The famine is so harsh that the Egyptians sell Joseph their livestock, then their land, then themselves, to Pharaoh. And “he moved the people to the cities, from one end of Egypt to the other.” Why tell us? Rashi, from the Talmud: to Joseph’s credit. If everyone was living somewhere new, no one could taunt his brothers as the only foreigners (Chullin 60b). It’s a kind motive inside a hard policy: a whole nation uprooted and made Pharaoh’s serfs. Within a few generations, another Pharaoh will enslave Joseph’s own family.', try: 'Think of a newcomer around you, at work, in your building, in your community. Do one small thing today so they don’t feel like the only stranger.' },
    ],
  },

  'Vayechi': {
    meaning: 'And he lived',
    summary: 'Jacob lives his last seventeen years in Egypt. He asks Joseph to bury him in Canaan, blesses Joseph’s sons Ephraim and Manasseh (putting the younger first), and gathers his twelve sons to bless each one. After Jacob dies, the brothers fear Joseph will finally take revenge, but he comforts them. The book of Genesis ends with Joseph’s death and his request that his bones be carried to the promised land.',
    lessons: [
      { title: 'Kindness that can’t be repaid', text: 'Jacob asks Joseph to bury him in Canaan with “kindness and truth.” Rashi explains: kindness to the dead is true kindness, because the one who does it expects nothing back.', source: 'Genesis 47:29 · Rashi' },
      { title: 'Bless your children', text: 'Jacob blesses Ephraim and Manasseh, and to this day parents bless their sons on Friday night: “May God make you like Ephraim and Manasseh.” They were the first brothers in Genesis who didn’t fight.', source: 'Genesis 48:20' },
      { title: 'Everyone gets their own blessing', text: 'Jacob blesses each of his twelve sons differently, “each according to his own blessing.” No two people have the same gifts, and no two need the same encouragement.', source: 'Genesis 49:28' },
      { title: 'Harm can be turned to good', text: 'Joseph tells his frightened brothers: “Am I in the place of God? You meant evil against me, but God meant it for good.” His forgiveness comes from humility: it isn’t his place to get even.', source: 'Genesis 50:19–20' },
    ],
    days: [
      { verse: 'Genesis 47:28', headline: 'In the Torah scroll, Vayechi begins without a break.', text: '“Jacob lived in the land of Egypt seventeen years.” Unlike every other weekly portion, in a Torah scroll this one runs straight on from the last, with only the space of a single letter. Why so “closed”? Rashi: once Jacob died, the eyes and hearts of Israel were closed by the misery of the slavery that began. The Midrash adds that these seventeen years were closed off from all the troubles of the world (Bereshit Rabbah 96:1). The old man’s last years were sheltered, and while he lived, his family was sheltered too.', try: 'Think of the person in your family or circle who quietly holds things together. Tell them today what their presence keeps away.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 48:16', headline: 'Jacob tells two different stories about the same hard life.', text: 'To Pharaoh, Jacob said his years had been “few and bad.” Now, blessing Joseph’s sons, he sums up the same life differently: “the God who has shepherded me all my life to this day, the angel who redeemed me from all evil, bless the boys.” Rashi: the angel who was sent to me in each of my troubles. Same years: Esau, Laban, Rachel’s death, Joseph lost for twenty-two years. What changed is who he’s talking to, and what he wants them to carry. Many Jewish children still say this blessing at bedtime.', try: 'Tell one younger person a story from your hardest year today, and tell it as the story of how you were carried through it.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 48:22', headline: 'At the end, Jacob called his prayers his sword and his bow.', text: 'Jacob gives Joseph an extra portion, Shechem, “which I took from the Amorite with my sword and my bow.” Rashi tells of a war: after Simeon and Levi’s massacre, the neighbors gathered to attack, and Jacob took up arms. Then Rashi adds that the sword and bow were “his wisdom and his prayer.” The Talmud says it outright: “my sword” is prayer and “my bow” is supplication, because the psalm says, “I do not trust in my bow, and my sword will not save me” (Bava Batra 123a). Looking back, Jacob counts prayer among his weapons.', try: 'Before you reach for force in a conflict today, an argument, a threat, pulling rank, try Jacob’s other weapon first: pray about it, or ask plainly for what you need.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 49:1', headline: 'The line we whisper after the Shema began at Jacob’s deathbed.', text: 'Jacob calls his sons: “Gather, and I will tell you what will happen at the end of days.” Then he doesn’t. The Talmud says he wanted to reveal the final redemption, and the Divine Presence left him. He feared one of his sons was unworthy, as Abraham had Ishmael and Isaac had Esau. His sons answered: “Hear, Israel,” our father, “the LORD is our God, the LORD is One.” Jacob replied, “Blessed be the name of His glorious kingdom forever.” Moses didn’t write those words, but Jacob said them, so we say them quietly (Pesachim 56a).', try: 'Say the Shema tonight, and at the whispered line, think of one person in your family whose faith you are carrying forward.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 49:22', headline: 'Joseph refused what wasn’t his, so envy couldn’t touch him.', text: 'Jacob blesses Joseph: “a fruitful vine, a fruitful vine <i>alei ayin</i>, by a spring.” <i>Ayin</i> also means “eye,” and the Talmud reads it as “above the eye”: Joseph’s descendants are beyond the reach of the evil eye. Why? Because “Joseph’s eye, which did not seek to feast on what was not his,” his master’s wife, is not ruled by the evil eye (Berakhot 20a). Rabbi Yochanan, a famously handsome man, said he didn’t fear envious stares because he was of Joseph’s seed. People who don’t covet are hard to wound with envy.', try: 'Notice one thing you keep looking at today that isn’t yours, someone’s life, partner or success. Look away on purpose, and name one thing that is yours.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 49:33', headline: 'The Talmud says Jacob our father never died.', text: 'Jacob finishes blessing his sons, “drew his feet into the bed, and expired, and was gathered to his people.” Rashi notices what’s missing: the word “died” is never used for him. The Talmud goes further. Rabbi Yitzchak quotes Rabbi Yochanan: “Jacob our father did not die.” Rav Nachman protests: did they eulogize, embalm and bury him for nothing? Rabbi Yitzchak answers from Jeremiah, where God promises to save Jacob and his descendants together: as his descendants are alive, so he is alive (Taanit 5b). A person goes on living in the people who carry him.', try: 'Do one thing today that a parent, grandparent or teacher who has died would recognize as theirs: a habit, a blessing, a kindness in their style.' },
      { verse: 'Genesis 50:21', headline: 'Joseph told his brothers that one candle can’t put out ten.', text: 'With their father gone, the brothers fear Joseph will finally take revenge. He weeps, “comforted them, and spoke to their hearts.” What did he say? Rashi gives two answers from the Midrash. One: before you came, people whispered that I was born a slave, and you proved I am freeborn; if I killed you now, they would say I boasted of my brothers and then killed them. The other: “Ten candles could not put out one candle; how can one candle put out ten?” (Megillah 16b). You tried to put me out and couldn’t. I won’t even try.', try: 'Someone who once hurt you is uneasy around you now. Say one sentence today that lets them stop bracing.' },
    ],
  },
});
